<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>They Came From the Museum, 2015</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;A pro-separatist propaganda video featured the restoration of an Su-25 and&amp;nbsp;old training plane captured from the Luhansk Aviation Museum, the latter taxiing around while the former just sat there. The overwhelming theory is that this has been a way for Russia to introduce airpower&amp;nbsp;while maintaining the tiny "Seconds turn their back to the duelists, and then swear under oath they saw nothing" level of plausible deniability. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Naturally, this was not the first time something like this has happened. In the Korean War, the small North Korean Air Force suddenly sported a&amp;nbsp;gigantic fleet of MiG-15s, many&amp;nbsp;crewed by distinctly non-Korean pilots. Similarly, in the Bay of Pigs invasion, the Cuban exiles mysteriously managed to scrounge up an air force by themselves. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This scenario will elaborate on that, and introduce a whole&amp;nbsp;"new" (if museum-piece aircraft can be called new) fleet of aircraft on behalf of the rebels. Limited by politics, the goal is to see how well the aircraft can perform.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Scenario by Coiler12&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;-NOTE: THIS SCENARIO IS INTENDED TO BE PLAYED WITH UNLIMITED MUNITIONS ENABLED. &lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
